# alo OS: where a person's words are handed to their agent.
#
# ADR 0109. The person's shell writes one line here — a request, as they typed
# it. systemd accepts the connection and starts one `alo-agent` as the **agent's
# own user**, which is the thing neither the shell nor the daemon can do: the
# shell runs as the person, and `alo-agentd` refuses to run as root (ADR 0001
# §2), so nothing of ours may change user. systemd may, and it is already
# trusted with that.
#
# **Only the person's group may open it.** That is the same protection
# `docs/contracts/daemon-protocol.md` relies on one door along and in the same
# direction: there the agent's group reaches the daemon, here the person's group
# reaches the agent. The kernel decides it before a byte is read.
#
# **Accept=yes, so one connection is one process is one turn.**
# `daemon-protocol.md`: *nothing names a turn* — which turn a message belongs to
# is answered by the connection it arrived on. A waiting agent would hold one
# turn open with nobody asking anything, or manage several and need a name for
# each, which is the number an agent could change. This makes the rule
# systemd's to keep rather than ours to remember.
[Unit]
Description=alo OS: where a person's words are handed to their agent
Documentation=https://github.com/aloworld-org/alo-os/blob/main/docs/decisions/0109-a-persons-words-reach-the-agent-through-a-socket-systemd-opens-as-the-agent.md

[Socket]
ListenStream=/run/alo-asking/socket
SocketUser=root
SocketGroup=alo
SocketMode=0660
Accept=yes

[Install]
WantedBy=sockets.target
